qa(None, "What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval, and what does coverage mean?",
   "A confidence interval says where the average outcome (or a model parameter) probably lies; it shrinks as the sample grows. A prediction interval says where a single new observation will probably lie; it must include the noise of one observation, so it does not shrink to zero. Coverage is the share of new observations that actually fall inside their interval: a 90% interval that contains the truth in 90 of 100 new sessions has correct coverage. Width matters too: a very wide interval is easy to make correct and not very useful.")
qa(None, "How did you build the Bayesian predictive interval and how well did it cover?",
   f"The Bayesian ridge of E2 gives a Gaussian predictive distribution N(mean, sd^2) for each held-out session, so the central interval is mean +/- z*sd (z = 1.28, 1.64, 1.96 for 80, 90, 95%). Within the leave-one-woman-out samples the median empirical coverage at the nominal 90% level was {mcl('pooled', 'bayes_predictive', 0.9)} (pooled, {npw}) and {mcl('long', 'bayes_predictive', 0.9)} (longitudinal, {nlw}), i.e. close to nominal. It is correct only as far as the model assumptions (Gaussian noise, one population) are.")
qa(None, "What is a jackknife-conformal interval and why use it?",
   f"It does not rely on the model's own variance. Inside each training set I refit the model leaving out one training woman at a time and recorded the absolute errors on her sessions; the interval for a new session is the point prediction +/- the (1-alpha) quantile of these errors. If the new woman is exchangeable with the training women this gives about the right coverage without any Gaussian assumption. It gave median coverage {mcl('pooled', 'jackknife_conformal', 0.9)} at the nominal 90% level in the pooled sample ({npw}) and {mcl('long', 'jackknife_conformal', 0.9)} in the longitudinal sample ({nlw}). The guarantee is approximate here (the quantile comes from leave-one-out models, the prediction from the model on all training women) and weaker for correlated sessions of one woman.")
qa(None, "Where was coverage off, and why?",
   f"Mostly when the intervals were used on a different population: trained on the {n_wom('cross')} cross-sectional women and tested on the {n_wom('long')} longitudinal women, the median coverage at the nominal 90% level was only {mcl('cross2long', 'bayes_predictive', 0.9)} (Bayesian) and {mcl('cross2long', 'jackknife_conformal', 0.9)} (conformal), and {mcl('cross2long', 'constant_gaussian', 0.9)} even for the interval without any predictors. The longitudinal women simply vary more than the training women, so any interval calibrated on the training women is too narrow; exchangeability, the assumption behind the conformal guarantee, fails. Within the same population a few individual cells were slightly off (listed in the results file; for instance some 95% intervals were conservative).")
qa(None, "Do hormones make the intervals narrower?",
   f"Not noticeably. Compared with an interval that ignores all predictors, the 90% Bayesian interval for phase plus hormones (set C, pooled, {npw}) has {calc('E4 mean width ratio C bayes pooled', wd('C'), '{:.2f}', E4C, 'mean width_ratio_vs_constant, pooled, set C, Bayesian, 90%')} times the width, phase alone {calc('E4 mean width ratio A bayes pooled', wd('A'), '{:.2f}', E4C, 'mean width_ratio_vs_constant, pooled, set A, Bayesian, 90%')} times, and with the questionnaires added {calc('E4 mean width ratio D bayes pooled', wd('D'), '{:.2f}', E4C, 'mean width_ratio_vs_constant, pooled, set D, Bayesian, 90%')} times. So the questionnaires make predictions a little more precise, phase and hormones hardly at all, which matches the low out-of-sample R2: the predicted range for a new woman is almost as wide as the whole spread of the outcome.")
